      "problem": "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x(x-3)^{3}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x(x-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x(x-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{\\cancel{x}(x-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{(x-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{3}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=3.$ Therefore, $x=3$ is the only vertical asymptote.",
