      "dom_id": "question-80695",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)({x}^{2}-x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+1=0\n$$\n\nThe last equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes.",
